\label{lem: gluing map}
The manifold $S^3_{0,\bullet}(W)$ is  diffeomorphic to \((P\times S^1)/_Q\), where $Q$ is the diffeomorphism between $\partial_0\times S^1$ and $\partial_1\times S^1$ defined by the matrix $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}
-1 & -1 \\
0 & 1
\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ in the standard peripheral bases.

Since the components of the Whitehead link have linking number zero, the longitudes defined by a Seifert surface for $W$ coincide with the canonical longitudes of the link components. This implies that the  manifold $S^3_{0,\bullet}(W)$, fibers over the circle, with fiber $\Sigma$ obtained by capping one of the boundary components of the fiber surface $\Sigma_W$ with a disc, and monodromy $h'$ obtained by extending the monodromy of $W$. By the explicit description given in Lemma~\ref{lem: whitehead is fibered}, we deduce that the monodromy $h'$ is a single right-handed Dehn twist along a non-separating curve $\gamma_0\subset \Sigma$. By cutting $S^3_{0,\bullet}(W)$ along the torus spanned by $\gamma_0$ via the monodromy, we obtain a manifold $M$ diffeomorphic to $P\times S^1$. More precisely, if we denote with $\partial_0$ and $\partial_1$ the boundary components of $P$ coming from the boundary of a tubular neighbourhood of $\gamma_0$ in $\Sigma$ and think of $\gamma_0$ as a boundary parallel curve in $P$, then 
\[
M=\frac{P\times [0,1]}{(\tau_0(x),0)\sim (x,1)}.
\]
By construction, in the standard peripheral bases of $\partial_0 P\times S^1$ and $\partial_1 P\times S^1$ of $M$, the homological action of the gluing map $f$ needed to recover $S^3_{0,\bullet}(W)$ is described by $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}
-1 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{smallmatrix}\right).$
The manifold $P\times S^1$ is diffeomorphic to $M$ via a diffeomorphism supported in a collar of $\partial_0\times S^1$. More precisely, let $A=S^1\times [0,1]$ be a collar neighbourhood of $\partial_0\subset P$, with $S^1\times \{0\}=\partial_0$, containing the curve $\gamma_0$. Since $\tau_0^{-1}$ is isotopic to the identity of $A$ relative to $S^1\times \{1\}$, we can find a level preserving diffeomorphism $H$ of $A\times [0,1] $
so that
\begin{itemize}
\item $H$ restricts to the identity on $A\times \{0\}\cup S^1\times \{1\}\times [0,1]$;
\item $H$ is $\tau_0^{-1}$ on $A\times \{1\}$;
\item $H$ restricts to a left-handed Dehn twist on $S^1\times \{1\}\times [0,1]$.
\end{itemize}
By plugging the above diffeomorphism in a collar neighbourhood of $\partial_0\times S^1$, we obtain a diffeomorphism $g$ from $P\times S^1$ to $M$ which in the standard peripheral bases is given by the matrix  $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}
1 & 1 \\
0 & 1
\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ when restricted on $\partial_0\times S^1$, and the identity on the other boundary components. In conclusion, the manifold $S^3_{0,\bullet}(W)$ is obtained by identifying $P\times S^1$ with $M$ via $g$ and then gluing the boundary components via the map $f$, and this proves the desired result.

\label{assum 1}
Unless otherwise stated, for the rest of this section we will assume that $K\subset S^3$ is either a non-trivial knot or that $K$ is the unknot and $n\neq -1, 0, 1$.
Moreover, if $K$ is the $(p,q)$-torus knot, or the $(p,q)$-cable of a non-trivial knot, we further assume  $pq \ne n$. Finally, we suppose that  $K'$ is a knot such that $S^3_0(K') \cong S^3_0(W_K^n)$.

\label{thm: wald and Hatc}
    Let $M$ be a Seifert fibered manifold with non-empty boundary, and
    suppose that $M$ is not homeomorphic to a solid torus, to $S^1 \times S^1 \times I  $ or to the twisted $I$-bundle over the Klein bottle. Then the Seifert fibration on $M$ is unique up to isotopy, and every essential annulus in $M$ can be isotoped to be vertical.

Of course, if the band $b$ can be isotoped inside a cabling annulus then $\widetilde{K}$ is an unknot. We consider the link $L=\widetilde{K}\cup J$, where $J$ denotes a meridian of the band $b$. Notice that the components of $L$ have linking number zero, 
 and hence $\widetilde{K}$ bounds some surface in the exterior of $J$. Let $S$ be such a surface of minimal genus. We can assume that $S$ intersects the disc $D$ bounded by $J$ in the tubular neighbourhood of $b$ only in one arc. In fact, since $S$ has minimal genus, it must be incompressible in the exterior of $J$ and one deduces, by an innermost argument, that up to isotopy $S$ must intersect $D$ only in one arc plus possibly some nested circles parallel to $J$. If there are such circles, then we can simply replace $J$ with the innermost one of them, and study a link isotopic to $L$, for which the minimal genus Seifert surface for $\widetilde{K}$ has the desired property.

We now determine the genus $g(S)$ of $S$.
First of all, notice that $g(S)>0$. In fact, if $S$ is a disc, then cutting $S$ along the arc of intersection with $D$ would produce two discs in $S^3$ whose boundaries are $K_0$ and $K_1$. However, this is not possible since $L_K$ is not the unlink.

We now show that $g(S)<2$. Assume that this is not the case, and observe that $E_{L}$ is an irreducible and boundary-irreducible $3$-manifold. Irreducibility follows from the fact that $L$ is not a split link, otherwise $\widetilde{K}$ would have to bound a disc in the exterior of $J$, and we have already shown that this is not the case; compressibility of any of the two components of $\partial E_{L}$ would have the same consequence.
Since $E_{L}$ is irreducible and boundary-irreducible, by \cite[Corollary~2.4]{gabaifol2}, we know that the surface $S$ remains genus minimising in its homology class in all but at most one filling (that we call \emph{exceptional)} of $E_L$ along the boundary component corresponding to $J$. Since $\widetilde{K}$ is the trivial knot in $S^3$, we know that the exceptional filling must be the one given by $\infty$-surgery on $J$. However, we now show that also the filling given by $0$-surgery on $J$ must be exceptional, hence leading to a contradiction. In fact, in the $0$-surgery on $J$, we can isotope the band $b$ so that it becomes disjoint from a cabling annulus $A$ (see Figure \ref{fig:band slide}). 
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.6\linewidth]{band_slide.pdf}
    \caption{How to remove intersections between the band $b$ and the cabling annulus $A$ in the $0$-surgery along $J$.}
    \label{fig:band slide}
\end{figure}
Since $\widetilde{K}$ is obtained by oriented banding, $b\cup A$ is a genus one surface and, by checking its intersection with generators of the first homology group of the exterior of $\widetilde{K}$ in $S^3_0(J)$, one sees that it represents
the same homology class of $S$.

Summing up, we have that $S$ must have genus one. Using again that $L_K$ is not the unlink in $S^3$, we have that cutting $S$ along its intersection with $D$ produces an incompressible annulus $A'$ in the exterior of $L_K$, with $\partial A'=L_K$. Lemma \ref{lem: every annulus is cabling} implies that $A'$ is a cabling annulus for $L_K$, and $S$ is obtained by gluing the band $b$ to $A'$. In particular, the band and $A'$ are disjoint.

Since $\widetilde{K}$ is the unknot, by Lemma \ref{lem: surfaces in solid torus}, the knot $\widetilde{K}$ bounds a disc whose interior is disjoint from $S$, and in particular from the band $b$. The annulus obtained by gluing the band to this disc is a properly embedded incompressible annulus with boundary $L_K$, and therefore is isotopic in $E_{L_K}$ to a cabling annulus. In particular, the band $b$ can be isotoped in it, thus concluding the proof.

\label{assum 2}
We assume that either $n\neq 0$ or that $n=0$ and $K$ is a non-trivial knot in $S^3$, and that $K'$ is a knot such that $S^3_0(K') \cong S^3_0(W_K^n)$. 
Moreover, if $K$ is the $(p,q)$-torus knot, or the $(p,q)$-cable of a non-trivial knot, we further assume  $pq \ne n$.

By Proposition \ref{prop: Sigma cap to F}, $N_{\widehat{\Sigma}}$ is diffeomorphic to the manifold obtained by cutting $S^3_0(W_K^n)$ along $F$. This is the result of gluing $E_K$ to the $3$-manifold $P \times S^1$, so that if $\partial_0,\partial_1,\partial_2$ are the three boundary components of $P$ and $ p \in \partial_2$, then $\partial_2 \times \{x\}$ is identified to the meridian of $K$, while the regular fiber $ \{p\}  \times S^1$ is identified to the $n$-longitude of $K$.

On the other hand, the exterior of the $(2,2n)$-cable of $K$ is obtained by gluing to $E_K$ the manifold $X$ given by the exterior in the standard solid torus $\mathbb{T}$ of two unlinked parallel copies of the core. Clearly, $X$ is diffeomorphic to $P \times S^1$. This diffeomorphism identifies:
\begin{itemize}
\item the meridian of $\mathbb{T}$ with $\partial_2 \times \{x\} \subseteq \partial P \times S^1$;
\item the meridians of the two parallel copies of the core to $\partial_0 \times \{x\}$ and $ \partial_1 \times \{x\}$ respectively;
\item the standard longitude of $\mathbb{T}$ to $ \{p \} \times S^1$. 
\end{itemize}
The gluing map used to obtain the exterior of the $(2,2n)$-cable sends the meridian of this solid torus to the meridian of $K$, and sends the standard longitude of the solid torus to a $n$-longitude for $K$. Since this is the same gluing map used to obtain $N_{\widehat{\Sigma}}$, the two manifolds are diffeomorphic.

Since $S^3_{0}(W_K^n)$ is diffeomorphic to $S^3_{0}(K')$, the proof follows directly from Lemma~\ref{lem: cutting is cable} and Lemma~\ref{lem: gluing map} and from the fact that, up to reversing the orientation of both components of $L_K$ we have
    \begin{align*}
    &m_{K_i} =-\partial_i\\
    &l_{K_i} =\{p_i\}\times S^1,\quad p_i\in \partial_i 
    \end{align*}
    for $i=0,1$.

\label{prop: gluing handlebody gives S3}
    Gluing $\Sigma_0 \times [0,1]$ to $\overline{S^3 \setminus H_{\alpha}}$  along $ \Sigma_0 \times \{0,1\}$ via the map 
    \[
    \psi = \operatorname{{id}} \times \{0\} \sqcup \varphi \times \{1\}
    \] yields $E_{K'}$. Moreover, there exists an extension $\widetilde{\psi}$ of $\psi$ on $ \partial (\Sigma_0 \times [0,1])$ such that gluing $\Sigma_0 \times [0,1] $ to $\overline{S^3 \setminus H_{\alpha}}$  via $ \widetilde{\psi}$ yields $S^3$.

Orient the arc $\overset{\circ}{m}_{K_0}$ with the orientation induced by $m_{K_0}$, and $D_{m}=m_{K_0}\times I$ with the product orientation. Since $\psi(m_{K_0})=-m_{K_1}$, the image of $\partial D_m$, oriented with the boundary orientation, via $\widetilde{\psi}$ is given by 
\[\overset{\circ}{m}_{K_0} \cup j_1 \cup j_2 \cup  \overset{\circ}{m}_{K_1},
\] where $j_1, j_2$ are disjoint properly embedded arcs in the boundary of the tubular neighbourhood of $\alpha$, and where $\overset{\circ}{m}_{K_1}$ is the oriented arc in $\Sigma_1$ corresponding to $m_{K_1}$. 

There exists a disc $\Delta$, properly embedded in $H_\alpha$, that contains $\alpha$ and whose boundary is $\widetilde{\psi}(\partial D_m)$, see Figure \ref{fig:Delta}. 
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{Delta.pdf}
    \caption{The figure shows the disc $\Delta$ and the band $b$ in $H_{\alpha}$, in a zoomed-in view of the region shaded in Figure~\ref{fig:handlebody}.}
    \label{fig:Delta}
\end{figure}
Gluing a neighbourhood of the compressing disc $D_m$ to $\overline{S^3 \setminus H_{\alpha}}$  coincides with gluing a neighbourhood of $\Delta$ to $S^3 \setminus H_\alpha$. 
Let $\widetilde{\alpha} \subseteq \Delta$ be an arc that extends $\alpha$ and connects $K_0$ to $K_1$.
Let $b$ be the band contained in $H_\alpha$ joining $K_0$ to $K_1$, whose core is $ \widetilde{\alpha}$, and that intersects $\Delta$ transversely and only in $\widetilde{\alpha}$. 
If $\widetilde{K}$ denotes the knot obtained by banding $L_K$ along $b$, then $H_\alpha$ minus a tubular neighbourhood of $ \Delta$ is a tubular neighbourhood of $\widetilde{K}$, thus concluding the proof.
